※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `std.string.parse_f64` answers the `f64` nearest the decimal number the bytes
※ write, a tie going to the one whose last bit is nought, and how many bytes the
※ number took.  These are the numbers that are hard to get right: half way
※ between two `f64`, written out to every digit and a little either side of it;
※ the smallest and the largest, and the edge of the subnormal numbers; more
※ digits than nineteen, which is where the fast way has to ask the slow one; and
※ every power of ten an `f64` reaches and some it does not -- written by
※ `format_int`, so that the program makes the text it reads.
※
※ What each should come to is what Python's `float` says, which is correctly
※ rounded, written in the bits.  The status says which kind of answer was wrong.

unit ¤size → ¤idx

